, AND WHAT IS NOT\n# Each body carries its own label. Mercury to Saturn and the Earth are VALIDATED against\n# JPL Horizons (Development Ephemeris DE441) within twice the Standish page's nominal\n# error. Uranus and Neptune from Table 1 exceed the page's figures against DE441 (by 2.0\n# and 5.2 times) and are MODELLED; so are Pluto, the Moon and the seven moons, which\n# were measured against Horizons but have no published bound. See tests/solar_system_horizons_reference.rs.\n#\n# The three links below add the Mars-to-Jupiter, Saturn-to-Earth and Europa-to-Jupiter\n# light times: interplanetary relay geometry, a ringed-planet downlink and a moon-to-planet\n# hop.\nkind = \"solar-system\"\nepoch = \"2026-09-28T00:00:00\"\nobserver = \"Earth\"\ntrack_points = 180\n\n[[links]]\nfrom = \"Mars\"\nto = \"Jupiter\"\n\n[[links]]\nfrom = \"Saturn\"\nto = \"Earth\"\n\n[[links]]\nfrom = \"Europa\"\nto = \"Jupiter\"\n","epoch":{"input":"2026-09-28T00:00:00","jd_tdb":2461311.500800722},"frame":"ICRF (equatorial J2000), heliocentric; metres, metres per second","label":"MIXED: each body carries its own label. Planet positions are VALIDATED against JPL Horizons within twice the Standish nominal error (Mercury to Saturn and the Earth from Table 1; all eight planets from Tables 2a/2b); Uranus and Neptune from Table 1, Pluto, the Moon and the seven moons are MODELLED. Light times are Newtonian, from the same positions.","table":"Standish Table 1 (1800 AD to 2050 AD)","observer":"Earth","bodies":[{"name":"Sun","class":"star","parent":null,"label":"origin","naif":10,"pos_au":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"rel_km":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"frame":"heliocentric","track_au":[[0.0,0.0,0.0]],"r_km":695700.0,"r_eq_km":695700.0,"gm":1.32712440018e+20,"j2":null,"rot_h":609.1199,"pole_ra":286.13,"pole_dec":63.87,"pm_deg":16.13,"dist_au":0.0,"period_d":null,"ecl_lon":0.0,"ecl_lat":0.0,"method":"origin of the heliocentric frame","error":null,"light_s":500.09,"range_km":149922629.0,"sun_sep":0.0,"shapiro_s":null},{"name":"Me